WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:06.730
Film Courage: In viewing your website for&nbsp;RED
REEL Video production, you have a bio saying

00:00:06.730 --> 00:00:13.460
“It was founded in 2009” and “It was
born out of the recession – because if you

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:19.550
can get a job out of college, you might as
well do exactly what you want to do.”

00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:24.289
Even though we’re supposedly in a robust
recovery, I know a lot of people are still

00:00:24.289 --> 00:00:28.910
feeling the effects of the recession. Can
you talk about creating your own job essentially?

00:00:28.910 --> 00:00:32.200
Alexandria Bombach, Director, Cinematographer,
Editor of ON HER SHOULDERS DOCUMENTARY film:&nbsp;I

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:42.829
graduated in 2008 and I went to business school
because I was filming since I was a kid and

00:00:42.829 --> 00:00:47.160
I bought my first camera when I was 13. I
don’t think it ever really occurred to me

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:55.600
that filmmaking could be a possibility, it
was just a way too cool for me. It didn’t

00:00:55.600 --> 00:01:03.370
even occur to me that it was ever going to
happen. That didn’t even cross my mind.

00:01:03.370 --> 00:01:09.409
I ran away from home when I was about 16 and
so there was kind of a pull-yourself-up-by-your-bootstraps

00:01:09.409 --> 00:01:15.329
kind of a big recognition of&nbsp;Oh, I need to
have stability in my life.&nbsp;So when I did

00:01:15.329 --> 00:01:20.399
go to college it was for business and focused
on that.&nbsp;

00:01:20.399 --> 00:01:25.109
I went to a liberal arts school called&nbsp;Fort
Lewis College&nbsp;in Durango, Colorado. We had

00:01:25.109 --> 00:01:33.039
amazing professors that let me do whatever
I wanted with projects and turn in videos

00:01:33.039 --> 00:01:42.829
and it was a great atmosphere to learn about
who I was and what I wanted to be. After graduating,

00:01:42.829 --> 00:01:48.740
there were not a lot of jobs. It was the peak
of everyone getting fired from places and

00:01:48.740 --> 00:01:59.520
so I decided I would do video production and
that started with wedding videos or tutorials.

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:04.100
I was really connected with the outdoor industry
at that time and so Osprey backpacks and things

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:11.100
like that where they needed me to do 4 by
3 videos on DV tapes.&nbsp; Youtube had barely

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:17.579
even started so it was just a different time
period and slowly gained confidence enough

00:02:17.579 --> 00:02:22.489
to make my first film and it just kind of
took off from there.

00:02:22.489 --> 00:02:28.519
Film Courage: I think you had made some short
films about inspiring people in the environmental

00:02:28.519 --> 00:02:29.519
space?

00:02:29.519 --> 00:02:35.719
Alexandria:&nbsp;Yes, I had&nbsp;made a film&nbsp;before
that called 23 FEET. It was about people living

00:02:35.719 --> 00:02:41.939
simply to do what they loved in the outdoors
and took that on kind of an unorthodox tour

00:02:41.939 --> 00:02:48.060
where the film is called 23 FEET because that
was the size of my&nbsp;Airstream.&nbsp;I created

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:55.590
a screen that came down from the Airstream
and had an outdoor city theater tour where

00:02:55.590 --> 00:03:05.930
in 30 different places around the West showed
the film outside. Places like&nbsp;Tuolumne Meadows

00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:11.830
outside of Yosemite to like 30 climbers, to
a blocked off street in Portland, Oregon with

00:03:11.830 --> 00:03:22.870
300 people. So it was an amazing, very unique
distribution strategy, but I had no idea about

00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:24.730
film festivals or anything like that.

00:03:24.730 --> 00:03:28.120
What I did find was that people would come
up to me after the screenings or write me

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:35.959
letters later about how they had decided quit
their day jobs and empty their rentals out

00:03:35.959 --> 00:03:41.909
and move into a van and just live the life
that they have always wanted to live. It was

00:03:41.909 --> 00:03:47.310
really jolting to get these letters from people
who had decided to change their life after

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:59.420
seeing a film I’d made and it really spoke
to how powerful films could be.

00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:32.949
Film Courage: Do you think if it hadn’t
been during the time of the recession, you

00:04:32.949 --> 00:04:38.560
would have been less apt to try that? Just
because you saw so many things that were falling

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:40.680
apart that we didn’t think were going to
fall apart?

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:43.781
Alexandria:&nbsp;Yes…it’s probably not good
to say but I might be the only person who’s

00:04:43.781 --> 00:04:50.419
thankful for the recession. Because I think
I could have very easily just gotten a marketing

00:04:50.419 --> 00:04:57.380
job somewhere if it would have been a different
time. It really forced me to think&nbsp;Okay,

00:04:57.380 --> 00:05:02.340
I could do a job I really don’t want to
do and make okay money or I could do exactly

00:05:02.340 --> 00:05:08.639
what I want to do and push myself.&nbsp;And that
was a scary, scary risk and leap to take but

00:05:08.639 --> 00:05:10.439
I’m glad it turned out that way obviously.

